```js
var actualCoverage = coverageData[path] || (coverageData[path] = { path, statementMap: ..., fnMap: ..., branchMap: ..., s: {...zeros}, f: {...zeros}, b: {...zeros} });
```

Wait, actually the standard nyc instrumented preamble is:

```js
function cov_xxx() {
 var path = "...";
 var hash = "...";
 var global = new Function('return this')();
 var gcv = "__coverage__";
 var coverageData = global[gcv] || (global[gcv] = {});
 var actualCoverage = coverageData[path] || (coverageData[path] = { path: path, statementMap: ..., fnMap: ..., branchMap: ..., s: {...}, f: {...}, b: {...} });
 ...
 if (!coverageData[path] || coverageData[path].hash !== hash) { actualCoverage = ... re-init ...}
 return actualCoverage;
}
```

Hmm, roughly: `if (!actualCoverage || actualCoverage.hash !== hash)` re-create. Since hash is same for identical source, shared. OK.

I could dig into the instrumented output to see. But maybe simpler: reproduce the anomaly with two test files: run test_writeSolutionAndLabels.js + test_writeSolutionAndDiagrams.js together and see counts.

